    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a> began with the conquest of the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Persian Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> shortly after 550 BC. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> and two Persian invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> defeated the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Battle of Plataea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mycale">Battle of Mycale</a> in 479 BC, then took Sestos and, in 478 BC, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantion">Byzantion</a>. The behaviour of the Spartan general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a> alienated many of the Allies and led to his recall.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> was eager to end its involvement in the war. Its king Leotychidas had proposed moving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, which the Athenian commander Xanthippus rejected: the Ionian cities had been Athenian colonies, and the Athenians would protect them. With the Spartan withdrawal, a congress was called on the holy island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a> to found a new alliance to continue the fight, which gives the league its modern name. Contemporaries called it simply "the Athenians and their Allies". According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>, its official aim was to "avenge the wrongs they suffered by ravaging the territory of the king." Members swore an oath of loyalty, and the Athenian politician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristides">Aristides</a> spent the rest of his life on the affairs of the alliance.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The league numbered between 150 and 330 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, largely around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a> and the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Members could either supply armed forces or pay a tax to the joint treasury, and most chose the tax. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> writes that this was their own doing: wishing to avoid service, they paid money, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> increased its navy with the funds while any revolt found itself without enough resources or experienced leaders.</p><p>The treasury stood first at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a>, where congresses were held within the sanctuary of the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>. In 454 BC <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> moved it to Athens, allegedly to keep it safe from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and Athens switched from accepting ships, men and weapons as dues to accepting only money. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> indicates that many of Pericles's rivals saw the transfer as usurping the funds for building projects. Tribute paid to the league financed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a> on the Acropolis, replacing an older temple, and many other expenditures unrelated to defence. Defeated rebels paid in land and resources: Thasos lost its land, ships and the mines of Thasos to Athens, and after the revolt of Mytilene the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> was redistributed to Athenian shareholders sent to live there.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> was the league's biggest source of military power and the dominant naval power of the Greek world. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a> the league expelled the remaining Persian forces from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, and in its first ten years Cimon forced Karystos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> to join, conquered the island of Skyros and settled Athenian colonists there. In 460 BC <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> led 250 ships to aid the Egyptian revolt of Inaros and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amyrtaeus">Amyrtaeus</a>; after four years the Achaemenid general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megabyzus">Megabyzus</a> defeated the rebellion and captured most of the Athenian force. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isocrates">Isocrates</a> put the losses at some 20,000 men; modern estimates place them at 50,000 men and 250 ships. In 451 BC Cimon led 200 ships to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and died during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of Citium, though the fleet won a double victory off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamis-cyprus">Salamis, Cyprus</a>, the last major battle against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, the Athenians lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tanagra">Battle of Tanagra</a> but won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-oenophyta">Battle of Oenophyta</a> and controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> except Thebes, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-coronea-447-bc">Battle of Coronea</a> in 447 BC led to its abandonment. The Long Walls joined Athens to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> from 431 BC, Athens suppressed revolts and conquered neutral Melos, putting its men to death and selling its women into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The league began as a dual offensive and defensive alliance, or symmachia, of autonomous states, with an assembly of representatives to shape its policy. Many democratic members owed their freedom from oligarchic or tyrannical rule to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, which gave Athens an overwhelming advantage in the voting. Over time Athens established complete dominion, and it came to call the members "all the cities Athens rules." It also ruled through the courts: matters within and between states were resolved in Athens by courts of Athenian citizens and enforced by the Athenian military.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a>, from 450 BC, set up kleruchiai, quasi-colonies tied to Athens that served as garrisons across the league's territory. A number of offices held the empire together: proxenoi fostered good relations between Athens and the members, episkopoi and archontes oversaw the collection of tribute, and hellenotamiai received the tribute on Athens's behalf. Athenian politics shaped the league's course. In 461 BC <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a> was ostracized and succeeded in influence by democrats such as Ephialtes and Pericles, and Athens turned from alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> to alliance with Sparta's enemies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The league achieved its principal strategic goal by expelling the remaining Persian forces from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> ceased to pose a major threat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> for the following fifty years. After the battle off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamis-cyprus">Salamis, Cyprus</a>, an understanding with Persia was reached, whether or not a formal Peace of Callias was ever made, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> turned its attention to Greece.</p><p>Within the league, the members lost their autonomy. The siege of Thasos marked the change of the league from an alliance into, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>'s words, a hegemony, and by the mid-fifth century BC it is conventionally called the Athenian Empire. Historians see the moving of the treasury in 454 BC as a key marker of that change, while warning against treating any single event as its main cause. The tribute enriched Athens itself: it reinforced the city's naval supremacy and embellished it with art and architecture, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a>. Beyond the Aegean, Megara left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian League</a> to ally with Athens, and a double line of walls was built across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-corinth">Isthmus of Corinth</a>. The threat the league posed to Spartan hegemony, with Athens's heavy-handed administration, prompted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>, which drew in much of the Greek world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rebellion ran through the league's history. Naxos tried to secede in c. 471 BC and is believed to have lost its walls, fleet and vote. Thasos defected in 465 BC, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> founded the colony of Amphipolis on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/struma">Strymon</a> river near its mines of Mt. Pangaion; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, facing the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helot</a> revolt in its history, refused it aid. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> and Megara revolted after 447 BC: Euboea was restored as a tributary ally, Megara permanently lost. A thirty-year peace between the Delian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian Leagues</a> lasted only until 431 BC.</p><p>By the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> only Chios and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> still contributed ships. Lesbos revolted and failed; the Athenians ordered the death of the whole male population of Mytilene, then rescinded the order and executed the leading 1000 ringleaders. Chios revolted last, after the Syracusan Expedition, inspiring all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> to revolt, though Athens suppressed these revolts. After 27 years of war the Spartans, aided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and by strife within Athens, defeated the Athenian empire, and the league was dissolved in 404 BC under the direction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a>. Athens revived it in part as the Second Athenian League, founded in 377 BC against Spartan and then Theban hegemony, but never recovered the full extent of its power.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> was the alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> led against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> from 478 BC. It drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> from the Aegean, but Athens turned the members' tribute and its fleet into an empire, moved the treasury to Athens in 454 BC and crushed revolts, until defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> dissolved the league in 404 BC.</em></p>

